Monks, it is just as if there were finger marks and a thumb mark seen on the handle of an adze of a carpenter or a carpenter's apprentice. That carpenter does not have the knowledge: 'Today this much of my adze handle has worn away, yesterday this much, and on other days this much.' Rather, when it is worn away, he just has the knowledge: 'It is worn away.' In the same way, monks, for a monk who lives devoted to mental development, he does not have the knowledge: 'Today this much of my taints has been destroyed, yesterday this much, and on other days this much.' Rather, when they are destroyed, he just has the knowledge: 'They are destroyed.'

Monks, it is just as if an ocean-going ship, bound with rattan bindings, having been in the water for six months, is hauled up onto the dry land during the winter. Its bindings, weathered by wind and sun and then soaked by a heavy rain, would easily rot and fall apart. In the same way, monks, for a monk who lives devoted to mental development, the fetters (saṃyojanas) are easily stilled and rot away."

The Seventh Discourse.
